ow that u and u⁻¹ lie in R. These powers are in ℚ⊂ℂ, so even negative exponents are defined and nonzero. The finite minimum argument and the two module inclusions are already proved in S004."}],"R005":[{"id":"BG001","kind":"mathematical","at":"/inference/argument/1/text","fact":"For modules over commutative rings, a balanced additive pure-tensor formula induces a unique additive map on the tensor product, and equality on pure tensors implies equality of maps. In the present maps, compatibility with the K factor makes the induced maps K-linear. Applying an additive lattice operator to the M factor commutes with these scalar maps; an R-linear operator on M_R also descends to M_R/pM_R and commutes with the quotient tensor maps.","grounds":[{"location":"/definitions/6","reason":"M_R is R tensor_Z M."},{"location":"/definitions/12","reason":"V_K is M tensor_Z K."},{"location":"/inputs/3/statement","reason":"All specified lattice operators preserve M and M_R; E_ij preserves M_R."},{"location":"/inference/argument/0/text","reason":"The maps and kappa compatibility are given explicitly."},{"location":"/inference/argument/1/text","reason":"The quotient maps are given explicitly and multiples of p tensor to zero."}],"justification":"The tensor product is generated by pure tensors modulo additivity and balancing relations. The displayed formulas respect those relations, so descend uniquely; composites fixing the generators are identity maps. Acting on the module factor commutes with changing scalar factors. For an R-linear A, A(pu)=pA(u), giving a quotient operator and the same compatibility on quotient generators."},{"id":"BG002","kind":"mathematical","at":"/inference/argument/2/text","fact":"If (a_i) over the finite index set I is an R-basis of an R-module L and kappa:R->K is a unital ring map, then (a_i tensor 1) is a K-basis of L tensor_R K. Every R-linear identity between operators on L remains an identity after scalar extension, with R coefficients mapped by kappa.","grounds":[{"location":"/inputs/4/statement","reason":"For the chosen b, a_i=b e_i is an R-basis of M_R."},{"location":"/inference/argument/0/produces/0","reason":"F001 identifies M_R tensor_R K with V_K compatibly with lattice operators."},{"location":"/inference/argument/2/text","reason":"The proof explicitly extends the basis coordinate maps."},{"location":"/inputs/3/statement","reason":"The R-linear restrictions of the matrix units and their identities on M_R are available."}],"justification":"Write u=sum_i c_i a_i; then u tensor d=sum_i a_i tensor kappa(c_i)d, proving spanning. Each coordinate functional L->R induces L tensor_R K->K and evaluates a_j tensor 1 to delta_ij, proving independence. Operator sums, compositions and scalar multiplication have these same operations on pure tensors, so preserve identities. This verifies the scalar-extension steps already displayed in S003-S004."},{"id":"BG003","kind":"mathematical","at":"/inference/argument/3/text","fact":"In a field K of prime characteristic p, an integer a has zero image exactly when p divides a. Hence if 0<=r<p, the integer r! has nonzero image in K, including 0!=1. A unital ring map R->K sends each unit of R to a unit of K, and therefore to a nonzero element.","grounds":[{"location":"/inputs/0/statement","reason":"p is prime and char K=p."},{"location":"/inputs/1/statement","reason":"0<=lambda<p, and the local index r lies between 0 and lambda."},{"location":"/inputs/3/statement","reason":"Each binom(lambda,j) used is a unit in R."},{"location":"/inference/argument/0/text","reason":"kappa is shown to be a unital ring map extending the integer map."}],"justification":"Divide an integer by p; its image is the image of its remainder. By the defining minimal positive characteristic, no remainder from 1 through p-1 is zero, so the kernel is pZ. Every factor 1,...,r is consequently nonzero; a finite product of nonzero field elements is nonzero. For a unit t with inverse s, kappa(t)kappa(s)=1, so kappa(t) is in